  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThe cold front continues to sag south through northern Kansas this \nafternoon as a weakening MCV treks southeast into central KS. There \nis a 10% chance for an isolated storm to pop up in north central \nKansas from this feature through early evening, but otherwise the \nforecast area remains dry.  Subsidence settles in this evening as \nthe drier airmass offers a welcome relief from the humidity as \ndewpoints fall back to the low 60s overnight under mostly clear \nskies. Mid clouds may increase from decaying showers and storms to \nthe west Thursday morning towards central Kansas, otherwise should \nremain a dry Wednesday and likely the coolest afternoon in the \nforecast period with readings in the 80s.\n\nSfc ridge axis slides eastward by Friday morning, as low level \ntroughing develops to the west, veering winds to the south and \nincreasing warm air advection into the weekend. Southerly wind \nspeeds are around 15 to 20 mph sustained, increasing to near 25 mph \nsustained on Sunday afternoon. H85 temps gradually increase in turn \nfrom 20C on Friday to around 30C by Sunday. Dewpoints also climb \nback into the upper 60s, combined with highs in the upper 90s result \nin max heat indices around 100 degrees Sunday through early next \nweek. Ensembles into next week have decent agreement on highs as the \nupper ridge is finally shunted eastward by a incoming upper low \nacross the northern plains. A cold front is forced southward, \nalthough there remain high discrepancies on the timing of the front \nand minor perturbations within the southwest flow aloft for \nscattered showers and storms throughout the region. Therefore modest \npops were kept in for Sunday night to early Tuesday across northern \nKansas, mainly during the overnight periods.",
